n-required item, try to remove it; if the set (with required) still contains all required (which it always does), then remove it permanently. So we remove all non-required items. Attempts count each test (removal attempt). At the end, reduced_items is the set of required items in the order they appear in the original items list. one_minimal is true because the minimal set is unique. That fits all cases.\n\nBut wait: if we remove all non-required items, we end with exactly the required set. That is indeed minimal because you cannot remove any required item (since they are required). So yes.\n\nLet's implement that. We need to be careful: the \"attempts\" is the number of test executions. In our algorithm, we test each non-required item once to see if removing it still causes failure. So attempts = len(items) - len(required). And we remove all that are not required. So reduced_items = [item for item in items if item in required_set]. That preserves original order. one_minimal = True.\n\nWe should also handle case where required is empty? But fixture has two items, so not empty.\n\nNow, does the algorithm depend on the order of items? Probably not. The attempts is straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, there could be a more sophisticated algorithm like \"minimize using Zeller's delta debugging\" which tries to reduce by halves. That would give different attempts. But which one is intended? The terms \"attempts\" and \"one_minimal\" hint at delta debugging. In delta debugging, we count the number of test runs. The \"one_minimal\" might indicate whether the minimal set is unique (i.e., no other minimal subset of same size). For delta debugging, the result is a 1-minimal set, but uniqueness is not guaranteed. So this field suggests we should check if there is exactly one minimal set after reduction. But again, if failure condition is \"all required present\", the minimal set is unique.\n\nI think the above is the safest simplest interpretation.\n\nNow, we need to produce Python source code for `solve(case)`. The case argument is the payload object. We'll access case[\"items\"], case[\"required_failure_items\"]. We'll compute as described.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case[\"items\"]\n    required = set(case[\"required_failure_items\"])\n    # attempt count: number of non-required items tested\n    attempts = len(items) - len(required)\n    # reduced_items: keep only required in original order\n    reduced_items = [item for item in items if item in required]\n    # one_minimal: is there only one minimal set?\n    # In this straightforward reduction, the minimal set is exactly the required set.\n    # Could there be another minimal set of same size? No, because any set containing all required must have at least size len(required).\n    # So unique.\n    one_minimal = True\n    return {\"attempts\": attempts, \"one_minimal\": one_minimal, \"reduced_items\": reduced_items}\n\nBut is attempts correct? If we do a linear pass, we test each non-required item. That's 8 attempts. However, if we test removal of a non-required item, we don't need to test the final configuration because we know it works. Each test is a removal attempt. So attempts = number of non-required items.\n\nBut maybe the algorithm is supposed to test the full set initially? That would add 1 more attempt? The schema says attempts integer. It might include the initial test. But typically delta debugging counts all test runs, including initial. In our case, we start with the full set (which is known to cause failure), then we test removal of each non-required. That would be 1 (initial) + 8 = 9? But if we already know the full set fails, do we count that as an attempt? The problem doesn't specify. Usually, delta debugging starts with the full set as the failing test case, and then attempts to reduce. The initial test is already done, so maybe we don't count it. The fixture might expect a specific number.\n\nLet's see if we can infer from the fixtures. Without expected outputs, we cannot be sure. Perhaps the problem is from a known coding challenge where the answer is deterministic. I'll search my memory: There's a well-known problem \"Delta Debugging\" where given a set of changes, find minimal set. The algorithm uses binary search and returns the number of tests performed. The \"one_minimal\" indicates if the result is unique.\n\nGiven that the fixture names are \"fg1-se-cr-02-b-000\" etc., maybe \"b\" indicates a variant. Possibly the expected outputs are standard.\n\nGiven the constraints, I'll implement the linear removal algorithm because it's simple and plausible.\n\nBut we should consider if the required_failure_items might be a superset of items that must be present? Or maybe they are the items that must be absent? No.\n\nAnother thought: The problem might be about \"test suite reduction\" where each item is a test case, and required_failure_items are the test cases that must be kept to cover certain faults. The reduced_items is a minimal set that still covers all faults. But we have no fault coverage matrix. So unlikely.\n\nI'll stick with the linear removal.\n\nNow, we need to output the implementation as a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and the Python source as a string.\n\nWe must escape properly.\n\nLet's write the solution.\n\nWe'll include comments maybe, but not required. Keep it minimal.\n\nAlso, ensure we don't mutate case.\n\nFinal code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case[\"items\"]\n    required = set(case[\"required_failure_items\"])\n    attempts = len(items) - len(required)\n    reduced = [item for item in items if item in required]\n    one_minimal = True\n    return {\"attempts\": attempts, \"one_minimal\": one_minimal, \"reduced_items\": reduced}\n\nWe must ensure that the returned object is JSON-serializable. It is.\n\nNow, embed this in JSON.\n\nThe output JSON should be: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    items = case[\\\"items\\\"]\\n    required = set(case[\\\"req